{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"display_name":"Python 3","language":"python","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.11.13","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"nvidiaTeslaT4","dataSources":[{"sourceType":"datasetVersion","sourceId":2424115},{"sourceType":"datasetVersion","sourceId":13289386},{"sourceType":"datasetVersion","sourceId":13293591}],"dockerImageVersionId":31154,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":5,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"id":"511ed738-15b9-499d-9048-2f24c62e4aa1","cell_type":"markdown","source":"# How Much of This Is Real? A Leakage-Audited, Explainability-Validated Benchmark for Federated MQTT Intrusion Detection\n\nThis notebook trains and evaluates intrusion-detection models on MQTTEEB-D (Aqachtoul et\nal., *Data in Brief*, 2025), a real-world MQTT attack dataset collected on a live IoT\ntestbed rather than a simulator. Real-world capture data is rare in this subfield -- most\nMQTT-IDS work (including the widely-used MQTTset) is generated from a simulated testbed --\nwhich is what makes MQTTEEB-D worth building a careful benchmark around rather than another\none-off accuracy number.\n\nThe three things this notebook is actually trying to establish, in order:\n\n1. **How much of a naive result on this dataset is leakage, not signal.** Fitting a scaler\n   before the split, shuffling rows randomly before a temporal split, and one-hot-encoding\n   the raw attack-payload text are all easy mistakes on this kind of data, and all three\n   inflate accuracy. Section 6.5 runs the naive version and the corrected version side by\n   side on the same data and reports the gap as an actual number, not an assertion.\n2. **Whether what the model learns matches domain expertise, not just a leaderboard score.**\n   The dataset authors already published a chi-squared feature ranking (their Fig. 5).\n   Section 8 checks the federated model's SHAP ranking against it -- agreement is a real\n   validity signal; disagreement is worth reporting just as honestly.\n3. **Whether a model trained here holds up on an independently-collected MQTT dataset.**\n   Section 10 evaluates the MQTTEEB-D-trained model zero-shot on MQTTset (Vaccari et al.,\n   2020), the most-cited prior MQTT IDS dataset. A real-vs-simulated generalization gap,\n   in either direction, is a publishable finding about how far these benchmarks actually\n   transfer.\n\nThe federated learning underneath all three (real FedAvg weight averaging, FedProx as a\nnon-IID comparison point, DP-SGD with an actual RDP-accounted privacy budget instead of\nnoise added to an already-trained weight delta) is necessary infrastructure for a credible\nevaluation, not the paper's contribution by itself -- plenty of work already establishes\nthat FedAvg/FedProx/DP-SGD are the right way to do FL. What this notebook adds is putting\nthat machinery through a real-world, leakage-checked, cross-dataset-tested evaluation\ninstead of the single held-out split most papers in this space stop at.\n\nRun top to bottom on Kaggle with the MQTTEEB-D dataset attached as an input. The first two\ncells will tell you immediately if a column name doesn't match what's expected here.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"bfc88996-2ac7-443d-bbe7-299ad440a554","cell_type":"code","source":"# This line is intentionally left active (not commented out). An earlier version of this\n# notebook shipped this as a commented-out \"uncomment if needed\" suggestion pinning\n# \"imbalanced-learn==0.12.3\" against an older scikit-learn -- that old imbalanced-learn\n# version imports a private scikit-learn module (sklearn.utils._metadata_requests) that\n# newer scikit-learn releases have since moved or removed, so on any Kaggle image with a\n# newer default scikit-learn already installed, the plain `import imblearn` a few lines\n# down crashed with \"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'sklearn.utils._metadata_requests'\"\n# -- and because the fix was commented out, it silently never ran and the crash kept\n# recurring on every fresh session. This is also a real, currently open Kaggle platform\n# issue independent of this notebook (see the Kaggle product-feedback thread linked below).\n#\n# The four versions below were verified together immediately before this notebook was\n# finalized (clean import, SMOTENC and SHAP's TreeExplainer both exercised against this\n# exact combination) -- see https://www.kaggle.com/product-feedback/579420 and\n# https://imbalanced-learn.org/stable/install.html (imbalanced-learn 0.14.2 declares\n# scikit-learn>=1.4.2). numpy/scipy are deliberately left unpinned here: forcing a numpy\n# version is what caused a *different* cascading break earlier in this project (installing\n# tensorflow-privacy once downgraded numpy and broke shap) -- pip will only touch numpy at\n# all if the currently installed one is too old for these four packages, which keeps the\n# blast radius small. If a later cell's `import tensorflow` breaks after this install runs,\n# that means this pip install did upgrade numpy out from under Kaggle's preinstalled\n# TensorFlow build; the fix in that case is to also pin TensorFlow explicitly right after\n# this line (e.g. `!pip install -q \"tensorflow==2.18.0\"`, matching whatever TensorFlow\n# version Kaggle's current image ships) rather than fighting the numpy version directly.\n!pip install -q \"scikit-learn==1.9.1\" \"imbalanced-learn==0.14.2\" \"shap==0.46.0\" \"xgboost==2.1.1\"\n#\n# dp-accounting is Google's standalone privacy-accounting library (the same RDP\n# accountant tensorflow-privacy uses internally). It's not preinstalled on Kaggle and\n# is needed for section 6a's actual (epsilon, delta) computation -- without it you can\n# still run the DP-SGD training, you just can't report a real privacy guarantee for it.\n# It's left commented out (optional) because the code below already degrades gracefully\n# (HAVE_DP_ACCOUNTING) if it's missing -- uncomment for a real privacy number.\n# !pip install -q dp-accounting\n\nimport os\nimport json\nimport random\nimport warnings\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\n\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler, OrdinalEncoder\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, RandomizedSearchCV, StratifiedKFold\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\nfrom sklearn.metrics import (\n    accuracy_score, f1_score, precision_recall_fscore_support,\n    classification_report, confusion_matrix, roc_auc_score,\n)\nfrom sklearn.utils.class_weight import compute_class_weight\nfrom sklearn.utils import shuffle\nfrom imblearn.over_sampling import SMOTENC\nfrom xgboost import XGBClassifier\nfrom scipy.stats import wilcoxon\nimport shap\n\ntry:\n    from dp_accounting import rdp as dp_rdp\n    from dp_accounting import dp_event\n    HAVE_DP_ACCOUNTING = True\nexcept ImportError:\n    HAVE_DP_ACCOUNTING = False\n\nimport tensorflow as tf\nfrom tensorflow import keras\n\nwarnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\", category=UserWarning)\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Config. Keep everything that affects the reported numbers in one place so a\n# co-author (or reviewer) can see exactly what was run without hunting through\n# ten cells for a stray constant.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRANDOM_STATE = 42\nN_SEEDS = 5                      # for the multi-seed comparison in section 7\nTEST_FRACTION = 0.20\nVAL_FRACTION = 0.10              # taken out of the remaining training pool\nSPLIT_STRATEGY = \"chronological\" # \"chronological\" or \"random\" -- see section 3\n\nNUM_CLIENTS = 5\nDIRICHLET_ALPHA = 0.5            # smaller = more non-IID across clients\nFED_ROUNDS = 15\nLOCAL_EPOCHS = 3\nBATCH_SIZE = 64\n\nUSE_DP = False                   # flip on for the privacy-utility tradeoff in section 6c\nDP_CLIP_NORM = 1.0               # per-microbatch gradient L2 clip norm\nDP_NOISE_MULTIPLIER = 0.8        # sigma relative to the clip norm\nDP_NUM_MICROBATCHES = 16         # see section 6a for why this isn't just \"batch_size\"\nDP_TARGET_DELTA = 1e-5           # standard choice: keep delta well below 1/N\n\nFEDPROX_MU = 0.0                 # >0 switches local training to FedProx (see section 6a)\n\nDATA_PATH = \"/kaggle/input/mqtteeb/MQTTEEB-D A Real-World IoT Cybersecurity Dataset for AI-Powered Threat Detection in MQTT Networks/MQTTEEB-D_Final_Dataset/Preprocessed_Data/MQTTEEB-D_cleaned_data.csv\"\n\nRUN_TAG = datetime.now().strftime(\"%Y%m%d_%H%M\")\nRESULT_DIR = f\"./results_{RUN_TAG}\"\nos.makedirs(RESULT_DIR, exist_ok=True)\n\n\ndef set_all_seeds(seed):\n    random.seed(seed)\n    np.random.seed(seed)\n    tf.random.set_seed(seed)\n\n\nset_all_seeds(RANDOM_STATE)\nprint(\"Results for this run will be written to:\", RESULT_DIR)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-13T03:06:09.047248Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-13T03:06:09.047562Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-13T03:06:40.232584Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-13T03:06:09.047517Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-13T03:06:40.231869Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"\u001b[2K   \u001b[90m━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\u001b[0m \u001b[32m9.3/9.3 MB\u001b[0m \u001b[31m92.3 MB/s\u001b[0m eta \u001b[36m0:00:00\u001b[0m:00:01\u001b[0m00:01\u001b[0m\n\u001b[2K   \u001b[90m━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\u001b[0m \u001b[32m236.1/236.1 kB\u001b[0m \u001b[31m19.3 MB/s\u001b[0m eta \u001b[36m0:00:00\u001b[0m\n\u001b[2K   \u001b[90m━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\u001b[0m \u001b[32m540.2/540.2 kB\u001b[0m \u001b[31m38.0 MB/s\u001b[0m eta \u001b[36m0:00:00\u001b[0m\n\u001b[2K   \u001b[90m━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\u001b[0m \u001b[32m153.9/153.9 MB\u001b[0m \u001b[31m12.3 MB/s\u001b[0m eta \u001b[36m0:00:00\u001b[0m00:01\u001b[0m00:01\u001b[0m\n\u001b[2K   \u001b[90m━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\u001b[0m \u001b[32m474.0/474.0 kB\u001b[0m \u001b[31m31.8 MB/s\u001b[0m eta \u001b[36m0:00:00\u001b[0m\n\u001b[?25h\u001b[31mERROR: pip's dependency resolver does not currently take into account all the packages that are installed. This behaviour is the source of the following dependency conflicts.\ncategory-encoders 2.7.0 requires scikit-learn<1.6.0,>=1.0.0, but you have scikit-learn 1.9.1 which is incompatible.\ncesium 0.12.4 requires numpy<3.0,>=2.0, but you have numpy 1.26.4 which is incompatible.\u001b[0m\u001b[31m\n\u001b[0m","output_type":"stream"},{"name":"stderr","text":"2026-09-13 03:06:29.836730: E external/local_xla/xla/stream_executor/cuda/cuda_fft.cc:477] Unable to register cuFFT factory: Attempting to register factory for plugin cuFFT when one has already been registered\nWARNING: All log messages before absl::InitializeLog() is called are written to STDERR\nE0000 00:00:1789268789.994494      40 cuda_dnn.cc:8310] Unable to register cuDNN factory: Attempting to register factory for plugin cuDNN when one has already been registered\nE0000 00:00:1789268790.043256      40 cuda_blas.cc:1418] Unable to register cuBLAS factory: Attempting to register factory for plugin cuBLAS when one has already been registered\n","output_type":"stream"},{"name":"stdout","text":"Results for this run will be written to: ./results_20260913_0306\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":1},{"id":"075ec5f6-7e23-446b-8d1b-a6f45b61e896","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 1. Load the data and check the schema before doing anything else\n\nThis is the step that got skipped in earlier drafts. The dataset paper's Table 2 lists a\nbinary `target` column (\"normal or under attack\") as well as the multiclass `label` column\nwe actually want to predict. If both end up in the CSV we load, and we only drop `label`,\nwe've handed the model an almost-perfect shortcut for the legitimate-vs-attack boundary.\nSo: print the columns, and refuse to proceed silently if something that looks like a leak\nis sitting in there.\n\nOne more thing checked right here, before anything else touches a column by name: this\npipeline's column names were written from the dataset paper's own tables, and a released\nCSV doesn't always match a paper's table byte-for-byte (this project already hit exactly\nthat: the paper's `mqtt_conflag_cleanness` versus a real header that may instead use\ntshark's standard `mqtt_conflag_cleansess` spelling, or dotted names like `mqtt.conflag.cleansess`\nif the CSV wasn't already converted to underscores). Rather than let a mismatch surface as a\nbare `KeyError` three sections downstream with no context, every column this pipeline\ndepends on is resolved against a short list of known spelling/notation variants immediately\nafter loading, and anything that still can't be matched stops execution here with the real\ncolumn list printed, so fixing it is a one-line edit to `COLUMN_ALIASES` below instead of a\ndebugging session.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"00bf7ec0-fe48-4c15-bf4b-7d65482308ac","cell_type":"code","source":"# canonical name this pipeline uses -> real-world spellings/notations seen for that column\n# across the dataset paper's tables, the released CSV, and MQTTset's tshark-derived schema.\n# Add to a list here if a real header uses yet another spelling -- nothing else needs to\n# change, since the rest of the pipeline only ever refers to the canonical name on the left.\nCOLUMN_ALIASES = {\n    \"timestamp\": [\"timestamp\", \"frame.time\", \"frame_time\"],\n    \"label\": [\"label\", \"Label\", \"attack_type\", \"class\"],\n    \"target\": [\"target\", \"Target\"],\n    \"tcp_flags\": [\"tcp_flags\", \"tcp.flags\"],\n    \"tcp_time_delta\": [\"tcp_time_delta\", \"tcp.time_delta\"],\n    \"tcp_len\": [\"tcp_len\", \"tcp.len\"],\n    \"mqtt_conack_flags\": [\"mqtt_conack_flags\", \"mqtt.conack.flags\"],\n    \"mqtt_conflag_cleanness\": [\n        \"mqtt_conflag_cleanness\", \"mqtt_conflag_cleansess\",\n        \"mqtt.conflag.cleansess\", \"mqtt.conflag.cleanness\",\n    ],\n    \"mqtt_conflags\": [\"mqtt_conflags\", \"mqtt.conflags\"],\n    \"mqtt_dupflag\": [\"mqtt_dupflag\", \"mqtt.dupflag\"],\n    \"mqtt_hdrflags\": [\"mqtt_hdrflags\", \"mqtt.hdrflags\"],\n    \"mqtt_kalive\": [\"mqtt_kalive\", \"mqtt.kalive\"],\n    \"mqtt_msg\": [\"mqtt_msg\", \"mqtt.msg\"],\n    \"mqtt_qos\": [\"mqtt_qos\", \"mqtt.qos\"],\n}\n# every one of these must resolve to a real column or the pipeline stops; these two are\n# fine to be genuinely absent (target doesn't exist in every release, and not every capture\n# keeps a timestamp column, though losing it forces SPLIT_STRATEGY=\"random\" in section 3)\nOPTIONAL_COLUMNS = [\"timestamp\", \"target\"]\nREQUIRED_COLUMNS = [c for c in COLUMN_ALIASES if c not in OPTIONAL_COLUMNS]\n\n\ndef resolve_columns(frame, aliases, required, optional):\n    rename_map, unresolved = {}, []\n    for canonical in required + optional:\n        candidates = aliases.get(canonical, [canonical])\n        found = next((c for c in candidates if c in frame.columns), None)\n        if found is not None:\n            if found != canonical:\n                rename_map[found] = canonical\n        elif canonical in required:\n            unresolved.append(canonical)\n\n    if unresolved:\n        raise KeyError(\n            f\"Could not find a column for {unresolved} under any known spelling \"\n            f\"({[aliases.get(c, [c]) for c in unresolved]}). \"\n            f\"This file's actual columns are:\\n{list(frame.columns)}\\n\"\n            f\"Add the real spelling to COLUMN_ALIASES above and re-run this cell.\"\n        )\n    if rename_map:\n        print(\"Resolved column-name differences from the expected schema:\", rename_map)\n    return frame.rename(columns=rename_map)\n\n\ndf_raw = pd.read_csv(DATA_PATH, low_memory=False)\ndf_raw = resolve_columns(df_raw, COLUMN_ALIASES, REQUIRED_COLUMNS, OPTIONAL_COLUMNS)\n\nprint(\"Loaded:\", df_raw.shape)\nprint()\nprint(df_raw.dtypes)\nprint()\nprint(\"label distribution:\")\nprint(df_raw[\"label\"].value_counts())\n\nLABEL_COL = \"label\"\n\n# Columns that would leak the answer if left in the feature matrix. `target` is the\n# obvious one per Table 2 of the dataset paper (binary attack/legitimate flag) -- if it's\n# present it gets dropped below, not silently ignored.\nKNOWN_LEAK_COLUMNS = [\"target\"]\npresent_leaks = [c for c in KNOWN_LEAK_COLUMNS if c in df_raw.columns]\nif present_leaks:\n    print(\"\\nDropping known leak-risk columns before modelling:\", present_leaks)\nelse:\n    print(\"\\nNo columns from the known leak-risk list were found (checked:\", KNOWN_LEAK_COLUMNS, \")\")\n\ndf_raw = df_raw.drop(columns=[c for c in present_leaks if c in df_raw.columns])","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-13T03:06:40.233896Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-13T03:06:40.234477Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-13T03:06:40.70398Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-13T03:06:40.234457Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-13T03:06:40.703006Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Resolved column-name differences from the expected schema: {'mqtt_conflag_cleansess': 'mqtt_conflag_cleanness'}\nLoaded: (222813, 13)\n\ntimestamp                 float64\ntcp_flags                  object\ntcp_time_delta            float64\ntcp_len                     int64\nmqtt_conack_flags          object\nmqtt_conflag_cleanness      int64\nmqtt_conflags              object\nmqtt_dupflag                int64\nmqtt_hdrflags              object\nmqtt_kalive                 int64\nmqtt_msg                    int64\nmqtt_qos                    int64\nlabel                      object\ndtype: object\n\nlabel distribution:\nlabel\nmalformed     105528\ndos            41802\nbruteforce     27377\nslowite        20090\nlegitimate     17557\nflood          10213\nName: count, dtype: int64\n\nNo columns from the known leak-risk list were found (checked: ['target'] )\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":2},{"id":"16678b56-ef2c-4bcb-9676-7860247b5a34","cell_type":"code","source":"def flag_suspicious_features(frame, label_col, mi_threshold=0.85):\n    \"\"\"Catch anything else that correlates suspiciously well with the label.\n\n    This isn't a substitute for actually understanding the columns, but it's a cheap\n    safety net: if some column has near-perfect mutual information with the label, that's\n    worth a manual look before it goes into the model, whatever it's called.\n    \"\"\"\n    from sklearn.feature_selection import mutual_info_classif\n\n    y = frame[label_col].astype(str)\n    y_codes = pd.factorize(y)[0]\n    candidate_cols = [c for c in frame.columns if c not in (label_col, \"timestamp\")]\n\n    numeric_candidates = []\n    encoded = {}\n    is_discrete = []\n    for c in candidate_cols:\n        col = frame[c]\n        if pd.api.types.is_numeric_dtype(col):\n            encoded[c] = col.fillna(0).values\n            # a numeric column with only a handful of distinct values is really a\n            # category (e.g. mqtt_qos); anything with lots of distinct values is a\n            # genuine continuous measurement and needs the continuous MI estimator,\n            # not the discrete one -- treating tcp_time_delta as \"discrete\" is what\n            # produced a bogus MI of ~1.0 the first time this was tried\n            is_discrete.append(col.nunique() <= 20)\n        else:\n            encoded[c] = pd.factorize(col.astype(str))[0]\n            is_discrete.append(True)\n        numeric_candidates.append(c)\n\n    X_check = np.column_stack([encoded[c] for c in numeric_candidates])\n    # normalize MI by the label's own entropy so 1.0 means \"fully determines the label\"\n    mi = mutual_info_classif(X_check, y_codes, discrete_features=is_discrete, random_state=RANDOM_STATE)\n    label_entropy = mutual_info_classif(y_codes.reshape(-1, 1), y_codes, discrete_features=True, random_state=RANDOM_STATE)[0]\n    mi_norm = mi / (label_entropy + 1e-12)\n\n    report = pd.Series(mi_norm, index=numeric_candidates).sort_values(ascending=False)\n    suspicious = report[report >= mi_threshold]\n    return report, suspicious\n\n\nmi_report, suspicious_cols = flag_suspicious_features(df_raw, LABEL_COL)\nprint(\"Top features by normalized mutual information with the label:\")\nprint(mi_report.head(8))\nif len(suspicious_cols):\n    print(\"\\nWARNING -- these columns look almost too good to be true (MI >= 0.85):\")\n    print(suspicious_cols)\n    print(\"Inspect these manually before trusting any downstream metric.\")\nelse:\n    print(\"\\nNothing crossed the 0.85 threshold. Doesn't guarantee there's no leakage, but it's a start.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-13T03:06:40.704989Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-13T03:06:40.705518Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-13T03:06:44.963766Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-13T03:06:40.705498Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-13T03:06:44.963003Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Top features by normalized mutual information with the label:\ntcp_time_delta            0.966578\ntcp_len                   0.439038\nmqtt_msg                  0.176516\nmqtt_hdrflags             0.156762\ntcp_flags                 0.103742\nmqtt_qos                  0.048852\nmqtt_conflags             0.010980\nmqtt_conflag_cleanness    0.010964\ndtype: float64\n\nWARNING -- these columns look almost too good to be true (MI >= 0.85):\ntcp_time_delta    0.966578\ndtype: float64\nInspect these manually before trusting any downstream metric.\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":3},{"id":"29059957-ca50-445d-9976-3c4e00b57d8d","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 2. Cleaning and feature engineering\n\nA few things worth doing deliberately rather than by accident, the way the earlier drafts\ndid:\n\n- `mqtt_msg` is raw MQTT payload content. Label-encoding it turns it into a near-unique ID\n  per row, and worse, the payload content here was literally generated by the attack\n  scripts (malformed packets, `rockyou.txt` entries for brute force, ...). A model that\n  leans on this column is learning \"this string appears in our attack generator,\" not a\n  generalizable network signature. We convert it to two derived, generalizable features\n  (length, character diversity) and drop the raw text.\n- hex-flag columns (`tcp_flags`, `mqtt_conflags`, `mqtt_hdrflags`, `mqtt_conack_flags`) get\n  converted to integers, but they stay *categorical* for the rest of the pipeline --\n  they're protocol flags, not continuous measurements, and that matters later when we\n  balance classes.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"6f29926a-71cb-43cd-8bac-7748da9d28ba","cell_type":"code","source":"CATEGORICAL_COLUMNS = [\n    \"tcp_flags\", \"mqtt_conack_flags\", \"mqtt_conflags\", \"mqtt_hdrflags\",\n    \"mqtt_conflag_cleanness\", \"mqtt_dupflag\", \"mqtt_qos\",\n]\nCONTINUOUS_COLUMNS = [\"tcp_time_delta\", \"tcp_len\", \"mqtt_kalive\"]\n\n\ndef hex_or_numeric_to_int(value):\n    if isinstance(value, str):\n        v = value.strip()\n        if v.lower().startswith(\"0x\"):\n            return int(v, 16)\n        if v.lstrip(\"-\").isdigit():\n            return int(v)\n        # not something we recognise as numeric -- leave it, LabelEncoder-ish handling\n        # downstream will deal with genuinely categorical strings\n        return np.nan\n    return value\n\n\ndef build_feature_frame(frame):\n    frame = frame.copy()\n\n    if \"timestamp\" in frame.columns:\n        # keep timestamp around under a separate name for the split, but it's not a feature\n        frame[\"_timestamp_for_split\"] = pd.to_datetime(frame[\"timestamp\"], errors=\"coerce\")\n\n    # derived, generalizable stand-ins for the raw payload text\n    msg = frame[\"mqtt_msg\"].astype(str)\n    frame[\"mqtt_msg_length\"] = msg.str.len()\n    frame[\"mqtt_msg_char_diversity\"] = msg.apply(lambda s: len(set(s)) / max(len(s), 1))\n    frame = frame.drop(columns=[\"mqtt_msg\"])\n\n    for col in CATEGORICAL_COLUMNS:\n        if col in frame.columns and not pd.api.types.is_numeric_dtype(frame[col]):\n            frame[col] = frame[col].apply(hex_or_numeric_to_int)\n\n    frame[CATEGORICAL_COLUMNS] = frame[CATEGORICAL_COLUMNS].fillna(-1)\n    frame[CONTINUOUS_COLUMNS] = frame[CONTINUOUS_COLUMNS].fillna(frame[CONTINUOUS_COLUMNS].median())\n\n    return frame\n\n\ndf = build_feature_frame(df_raw)\nFEATURE_COLUMNS = CATEGORICAL_COLUMNS + CONTINUOUS_COLUMNS + [\"mqtt_msg_length\", \"mqtt_msg_char_diversity\"]\nFEATURE_COLUMNS = [c for c in FEATURE_COLUMNS if c in df.columns]\nprint(\"Feature columns going into the models:\", FEATURE_COLUMNS)\nprint(\"Dropped from modelling: timestamp (kept for splitting), mqtt_msg (replaced by derived features)\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-13T03:06:44.96552Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-13T03:06:44.965772Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-13T03:06:45.793795Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-13T03:06:44.965756Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-13T03:06:45.793106Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Feature columns going into the models: ['tcp_flags', 'mqtt_conack_flags', 'mqtt_conflags', 'mqtt_hdrflags', 'mqtt_conflag_cleanness', 'mqtt_dupflag', 'mqtt_qos', 'tcp_time_delta', 'tcp_len', 'mqtt_kalive', 'mqtt_msg_length', 'mqtt_msg_char_diversity']\nDropped from modelling: timestamp (kept for splitting), mqtt_msg (replaced by derived features)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":4},{"id":"41a069ff-2218-4094-8b47-87931c4ddacf","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 3. Train / validation / test split\n\nEvery earlier draft shuffled all rows and split 80/20. That puts packets that are\nmilliseconds apart -- same TCP session, same attack burst -- on both sides of the split,\nwhich inflates every metric because the test set isn't really \"unseen\" traffic. Here we\nsort by timestamp and cut chronologically: the model is trained on the earlier part of the\ncapture and tested on the later part, which is a much closer match to how it would actually\nbe deployed (detect attacks it hasn't seen play out yet, not attacks eight packets away from\na training example).\n\nIf your Kaggle dataset also includes the individual per-loop CSVs\n(`MQTTEEB-D_dataset_loop_1..4.csv`), a cross-loop split (train on loops 1-3, test on loop 4)\nis even more convincing -- see the commented-out block below.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"81613a35-aa38-4f5d-944b-f53eb3a1f2b6","cell_type":"code","source":"def chronological_split(frame, label_col, test_fraction, val_fraction, feature_columns):\n    frame = frame.sort_values(\"_timestamp_for_split\").reset_index(drop=True)\n    n = len(frame)\n    n_test = int(n * test_fraction)\n    n_val = int(n * val_fraction)\n\n    test_df = frame.iloc[n - n_test:]\n    val_df = frame.iloc[n - n_test - n_val: n - n_test]\n    train_df = frame.iloc[: n - n_test - n_val]\n\n    def split_xy(d):\n        return d[feature_columns].copy(), d[label_col].astype(str).values\n\n    return split_xy(train_df), split_xy(val_df), split_xy(test_df)\n\n\ndef random_split(frame, label_col, test_fraction, val_fraction, feature_columns, seed):\n    y_all = frame[label_col].astype(str)\n    train_val_df, test_df = train_test_split(\n        frame, test_size=test_fraction, random_state=seed, stratify=y_all)\n    y_train_val = train_val_df[label_col].astype(str)\n    train_df, val_df = train_test_split(\n        train_val_df, test_size=val_fraction / (1 - test_fraction),\n        random_state=seed, stratify=y_train_val)\n\n    def split_xy(d):\n        return d[feature_columns].copy(), d[label_col].astype(str).values\n\n    return split_xy(train_df), split_xy(val_df), split_xy(test_df)\n\n\nif SPLIT_STRATEGY == \"chronological\":\n    (X_train_raw, y_train_raw), (X_val_raw, y_val_raw), (X_test_raw, y_test_raw) = chronological_split(\n        df, LABEL_COL, TEST_FRACTION, VAL_FRACTION, FEATURE_COLUMNS)\nelse:\n    (X_train_raw, y_train_raw), (X_val_raw, y_val_raw), (X_test_raw, y_test_raw) = random_split(\n        df, LABEL_COL, TEST_FRACTION, VAL_FRACTION, FEATURE_COLUMNS, RANDOM_STATE)\n\nprint(f\"split strategy: {SPLIT_STRATEGY}\")\nprint(\"train:\", X_train_raw.shape, Counter(y_train_raw))\nprint(\"val:  \", X_val_raw.shape, Counter(y_val_raw))\nprint(\"test: \", X_test_raw.shape, Counter(y_test_raw))\n\n# --- optional cross-loop generalization check -------------------------------------------\n# Uncomment if the per-loop files are attached as a separate Kaggle input. This trains on\n# loops 1-3 and tests on loop 4 in its entirety, which is the strongest generalization test\n# this dataset supports (a whole attack execution the model has never seen any part of).\n#\n# loop_paths = sorted(glob.glob(\"/kaggle/input/mqtteeb/**/MQTTEEB-D_dataset_loop_*.csv\", recursive=True))\n# if loop_paths:\n#     loops = {p: build_feature_frame(pd.read_csv(p, low_memory=False)) for p in loop_paths}\n#     ... concatenate loops 1-3 for training, hold out loop 4 for test, same feature columns","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-13T03:06:45.794564Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-13T03:06:45.794801Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-13T03:06:45.902658Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-13T03:06:45.794782Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-13T03:06:45.901673Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"split strategy: chronological\ntrain: (155970, 12) Counter({'malformed': 73399, 'dos': 30198, 'bruteforce': 18905, 'slowite': 13146, 'legitimate': 12629, 'flood': 7493, 'nan': 200})\nval:   (22281, 12) Counter({'malformed': 10419, 'dos': 3859, 'bruteforce': 2751, 'slowite': 2615, 'legitimate': 1574, 'flood': 1039, 'nan': 24})\ntest:  (44562, 12) Counter({'malformed': 21710, 'dos': 7745, 'bruteforce': 5721, 'slowite': 4329, 'legitimate': 3354, 'flood': 1681, 'nan': 22})\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":5},{"id":"a1fe3fa1-da22-405f-9216-58f61a4467de","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 4. Label encoding and preprocessing fit on the training fold only\n\nThe scaler and the categorical encoder are both fit on `X_train_raw` alone and then applied\nto validation/test. This sounds obvious but the most \"finished-looking\" of the earlier\ndrafts fit `StandardScaler` on the full dataset before splitting, which is the single\nbiggest reason not to trust its reported numbers.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"60839387-6909-462b-9a8f-368f38a1ad3d","cell_type":"code","source":"label_encoder_classes = sorted(pd.unique(np.concatenate([y_train_raw, y_val_raw, y_test_raw])))\nlabel_to_idx = {c: i for i, c in enumerate(label_encoder_classes)}\nidx_to_label = {i: c for c, i in label_to_idx.items()}\nprint(\"Classes:\", label_encoder_classes)\n\ny_train = np.array([label_to_idx[c] for c in y_train_raw])\ny_val = np.array([label_to_idx[c] for c in y_val_raw])\ny_test = np.array([label_to_idx[c] for c in y_test_raw])\n\ncat_idx = [FEATURE_COLUMNS.index(c) for c in CATEGORICAL_COLUMNS if c in FEATURE_COLUMNS]\ncont_idx = [FEATURE_COLUMNS.index(c) for c in FEATURE_COLUMNS if c not in CATEGORICAL_COLUMNS]\n\nscaler = StandardScaler()\nX_train_cont = scaler.fit_transform(X_train_raw.iloc[:, cont_idx])\nX_val_cont = scaler.transform(X_val_raw.iloc[:, cont_idx])\nX_test_cont = scaler.transform(X_test_raw.iloc[:, cont_idx])\n\n# categorical columns are already small-integer coded (flags), so we leave them as-is\n# rather than one-hot-encoding into a much wider, sparser matrix\nX_train_cat = X_train_raw.iloc[:, cat_idx].values.astype(float)\nX_val_cat = X_val_raw.iloc[:, cat_idx].values.astype(float)\nX_test_cat = X_test_raw.iloc[:, cat_idx].values.astype(float)\n\nX_train = np.hstack([X_train_cat, X_train_cont])\nX_val = np.hstack([X_val_cat, X_val_cont])\nX_test = np.hstack([X_test_cat, X_test_cont])\nordered_feature_names = [FEATURE_COLUMNS[i] for i in cat_idx] + [FEATURE_COLUMNS[i] for i in cont_idx]\nsmote_categorical_mask = [True] * len(cat_idx) + [False] * len(cont_idx)\n\nprint(\"Final feature matrix shapes:\", X_train.shape, X_val.shape, X_test.shape)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-13T03:06:45.903673Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-13T03:06:45.903947Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-13T03:06:45.989186Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-13T03:06:45.903923Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-13T03:06:45.988333Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Classes: ['bruteforce', 'dos', 'flood', 'legitimate', 'malformed', 'nan', 'slowite']\nFinal feature matrix shapes: (155970, 12) (22281, 12) (44562, 12)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":6},{"id":"2f50753e-d653-4f6d-907b-8b358ed92d3f","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 5. Class balancing, done on the training fold only, respecting categorical columns\n\nPlain SMOTE interpolates linearly between neighbours, which is fine for continuous features\nbut produces nonsense for protocol flags (a QoS of 1.4 doesn't mean anything on the wire).\n`SMOTENC` handles this properly: continuous features get interpolated as usual, categorical\nones are taken from one of the real neighbours instead of averaged.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"2fe1518d-4d67-4360-b608-7d52f4baeb14","cell_type":"code","source":"print(\"Before balancing:\", Counter(y_train))\n\nsmote = SMOTENC(categorical_features=smote_categorical_mask, random_state=RANDOM_STATE)\nX_train_bal, y_train_bal = smote.fit_resample(X_train, y_train)\n\nprint(\"After balancing: \", Counter(y_train_bal))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-13T03:06:45.989973Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-13T03:06:45.990246Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-13T03:07:50.494235Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-13T03:06:45.990227Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-13T03:07:50.493346Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Before balancing: Counter({4: 73399, 1: 30198, 0: 18905, 6: 13146, 3: 12629, 2: 7493, 5: 200})\nAfter balancing:  Counter({3: 73399, 1: 73399, 6: 73399, 4: 73399, 5: 73399, 0: 73399, 2: 73399})\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":7},{"id":"6c42caf7-4b84-44a6-8271-c4837e3ca4f1","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 6. Centralized baselines: Random Forest and XGBoost\n\nThese are here for two reasons: they're a reasonable ceiling to compare the neural /\nfederated models against, and their feature importances (Section 8) are what we check\nagainst the dataset paper's own chi-squared results.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"d02a8834-a959-4db7-bed6-01b10dbf300a","cell_type":"code","source":"def tune_and_fit(estimator, param_dist, X, y, n_iter=20, cv_folds=4):\n    # n_jobs=-1 lives on the search here, not on the estimator passed in (see the RF/XGB\n    # constructors below) -- this matters more than it looks like it should. RandomizedSearchCV\n    # already parallelizes across n_iter * cv_folds model fits using every core; if the\n    # estimator *also* asks for every core per fit, you get dozens of processes each trying to\n    # claim the whole machine at once. That oversubscription doesn't just fail to help, it\n    # actively thrashes (constant context-switching between far more threads than there are\n    # cores), which is what turns a search that should take minutes into one that looks hung\n    # for a very long time with nothing printed -- exactly what \"stuck on Tuning Random Forest\"\n    # usually is. verbose=2 below also means this now prints a line per fit, so progress is\n    # visible instead of the run looking frozen either way.\n    search = RandomizedSearchCV(\n        estimator, param_distributions=param_dist, n_iter=n_iter, scoring=\"f1_macro\",\n        cv=StratifiedKFold(n_splits=cv_folds, shuffle=True, random_state=RANDOM_STATE),\n        random_state=RANDOM_STATE, n_jobs=-1, verbose=2,\n    )\n    search.fit(X, y)\n    print(f\"  best params: {search.best_params_}  (cv f1_macro={search.best_score_:.4f})\")\n    return search.best_estimator_\n\n\nrf_param_dist = {\n    \"n_estimators\": [200, 300, 400, 600],\n    \"max_depth\": [12, 16, 20, 30],  # capped, not unbounded (None) -- an unbounded-depth tree\n                                     # at 600 estimators on a SMOTE-balanced multi-hundred-\n                                     # thousand-row training set is comfortably the single\n                                     # slowest possible draw from this grid, for a class of\n                                     # tabular flow features that rarely benefits from depth\n                                     # past ~30 anyway; 30 still lets very deep trees compete\n                                     # without that pathological worst case\n    \"min_samples_leaf\": [1, 2, 4],\n}\nprint(\"Tuning Random Forest...\")\n# If this is still slow on the full dataset even with the n_jobs fix above (a few hundred\n# thousand rows after SMOTE-NC balancing is a lot more than the small synthetic set this\n# pipeline was smoke-tested against), the next lever is n_iter -- tune_and_fit(..., n_iter=8)\n# searches fewer combinations, or drop None from max_depth above to rule out unbounded trees\n# on the largest n_estimators draws, which are the single slowest combination in this grid.\nrf_model = tune_and_fit(\n    RandomForestClassifier(random_state=RANDOM_STATE, n_jobs=1),\n    rf_param_dist, X_train_bal, y_train_bal,\n)\n\nxgb_param_dist = {\n    \"learning_rate\": [0.02, 0.05, 0.08, 0.1],\n    \"max_depth\": [4, 6, 8],\n    \"n_estimators\": [200, 300, 400],\n    \"subsample\": [0.7, 0.85, 1.0],\n    \"colsample_bytree\": [0.7, 0.85, 1.0],\n}\nprint(\"Tuning XGBoost...\")\nxgb_model = tune_and_fit(\n    XGBClassifier(\n        objective=\"multi:softprob\", num_class=len(label_encoder_classes),\n        tree_method=\"hist\", eval_metric=\"mlogloss\", random_state=RANDOM_STATE, n_jobs=1,\n    ),\n    xgb_param_dist, X_train_bal, y_train_bal,\n)\n\n\ndef report_on_test(model, name):\n    preds = model.predict(X_test)\n    acc = accuracy_score(y_test, preds)\n    f1_macro = f1_score(y_test, preds, average=\"macro\")\n    print(f\"\\n{name}: accuracy={acc:.4f}  macro-F1={f1_macro:.4f}\")\n    print(classification_report(y_test, preds, target_names=label_encoder_classes, digits=4))\n    return {\"model\": name, \"accuracy\": acc, \"f1_macro\": f1_macro}\n\n\nbaseline_results = [\n    report_on_test(rf_model, \"Random Forest (centralized)\"),\n    report_on_test(xgb_model, \"XGBoost (centralized)\"),\n]","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-13T03:07:50.495137Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-13T03:07:50.495421Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-13T05:32:15.098345Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-13T03:07:50.49539Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-13T05:32:15.097335Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Tuning Random Forest...\nFitting 4 folds for each of 20 candidates, totalling 80 fits\n[CV] END .max_depth=20, min_samples_leaf=1, n_estimators=600; total time= 8.4min\n[CV] END .max_depth=30, min_samples_leaf=2, n_estimators=200; total time= 2.9min\n[CV] END .max_depth=20, min_samples_leaf=1, n_estimators=400; total time= 5.7min\n[CV] END .max_depth=30, min_samples_leaf=2, n_estimators=600; total time= 8.4min\n[CV] END .max_depth=20, min_samples_leaf=1, n_estimators=600; total time= 8.4min\n[CV] END .max_depth=30, min_samples_leaf=2, n_estimators=200; total time= 2.9min\n[CV] END .max_depth=20, min_samples_leaf=1, n_estimators=400; total time= 5.7min\n[CV] END .max_depth=30, min_samples_leaf=2, n_estimators=600; total time= 8.4min\n[CV] END .max_depth=20, min_samples_leaf=1, n_estimators=600; total time= 8.5min\n[CV] END .max_depth=30, min_samples_leaf=2, n_estimators=200; total time= 2.8min\n[CV] END .m