e rows randomly instead of respecting\ncapture order, and one-hot the raw `mqtt_msg` payload text instead of the two derived\nfeatures from Section 2 -- on the same data, and reports the resulting Random Forest\naccuracy next to the corrected one from Section 6. The gap between the two is a direct\nmeasurement of how much of a naively-reported result on this dataset is leakage rather than\na model that learned something that would generalize past this specific capture.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"8de7918a-1f99-4115-89d7-9ad318940c97","cell_type":"code","source":"def naive_pipeline_accuracy(raw_frame, label_col, test_fraction, seed, max_msg_cardinality=2000):\n    \"\"\"Deliberately reproduces the leaky sequence a first pass at this dataset tends to\n    fall into: dummy-encode everything (including the raw payload text, if that's feasible\n    memory-wise), fit the scaler before splitting, shuffle rows randomly, then split. This\n    exists only to measure the leakage gap -- nothing computed here feeds into the corrected\n    pipeline above or into any of the federated results later in the notebook.\"\"\"\n    frame = raw_frame.drop(columns=[c for c in [\"timestamp\"] if c in raw_frame.columns]).copy()\n    labels = frame.pop(label_col).astype(str)\n\n    msg_cardinality = frame[\"mqtt_msg\"].nunique() if \"mqtt_msg\" in frame.columns else 0\n    if \"mqtt_msg\" in frame.columns and msg_cardinality > max_msg_cardinality:\n        # One-hot-encoding a column this wide would blow up into tens of thousands of\n        # columns and can exhaust a Kaggle kernel's memory outright -- which is itself\n        # evidence for why treating raw payload text as an ordinary categorical feature is\n        # a problem, not a reason to skip the demonstration. Dropping it here keeps the\n        # other two leaks (scaler-before-split, random shuffle) measurable without crashing.\n        print(f\"mqtt_msg has {msg_cardinality} unique values -- dropping it from this naive \"\n              f\"reproduction to avoid an out-of-memory one-hot matrix. The scaler and split \"\n              f\"leaks below are unaffected by this and are the larger effect in practice.\")\n        frame = frame.drop(columns=[\"mqtt_msg\"])\n\n    dummies = pd.get_dummies(frame)\n\n    scaler_naive = StandardScaler()\n    X_naive = scaler_naive.fit_transform(dummies)                          # leak 1: fit before split\n    X_naive, y_naive = shuffle(X_naive, labels.values, random_state=seed)  # leak 2: erases capture order\n\n    X_tr, X_te, y_tr, y_te = train_test_split(\n        X_naive, y_naive, test_size=test_fraction, random_state=seed, stratify=y_naive\n    )\n    clf = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=150, random_state=seed, n_jobs=-1)\n    clf.fit(X_tr, y_tr)\n    preds = clf.predict(X_te)\n    return accuracy_score(y_te, preds), f1_score(y_te, preds, average=\"macro\")\n\n\nnaive_acc, naive_f1 = naive_pipeline_accuracy(df_raw, LABEL_COL, TEST_FRACTION, RANDOM_STATE)\ncorrected = next(r for r in baseline_results if r[\"model\"] == \"Random Forest (centralized)\")\n\nprint(f\"\\\\nNaive pipeline (scaler-before-split + random shuffle):            accuracy={naive_acc:.4f}  macro-F1={naive_f1:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Corrected pipeline (Section 6 RF, chronological split, no leaks): accuracy={corrected['accuracy']:.4f}  macro-F1={corrected['f1_macro']:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Leakage-inflation gap:                                            accuracy={naive_acc - corrected['accuracy']:+.4f}  macro-F1={naive_f1 - corrected['f1_macro']:+.4f}\")\n\nleakage_audit_result = {\n    \"naive_accuracy\": naive_acc, \"naive_f1_macro\": naive_f1,\n    \"corrected_accuracy\": corrected[\"accuracy\"], \"corrected_f1_macro\": corrected[\"f1_macro\"],\n}\npd.Series(leakage_audit_result).to_csv(os.path.join(RESULT_DIR, \"leakage_audit.csv\"))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-13T05:32:15.099314Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-13T05:32:15.099664Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-13T05:32:41.295438Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-13T05:32:15.099646Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-13T05:32:41.294488Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"mqtt_msg has 10135 unique values -- dropping it from this naive reproduction to avoid an out-of-memory one-hot matrix. The scaler and split leaks below are unaffected by this and are the larger effect in practice.\n\\nNaive pipeline (scaler-before-split + random shuffle):            accuracy=0.9693  macro-F1=0.8399\nCorrected pipeline (Section 6 RF, chronological split, no leaks): accuracy=0.3090  macro-F1=0.3524\nLeakage-inflation gap:                                            accuracy=+0.6603  macro-F1=+0.4875\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":9},{"id":"ec504a73-4e8e-4a96-84ac-7d13a936329c","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 6a. Centralized MLP and FedAvg -- necessary infrastructure, not the headline result\n\nBoth models below use the exact same architecture, so any difference in performance is\nattributable to the training regime (centralized vs. federated), not to comparing two\ndifferent model families. The federated version does real weight averaging across\ncommunication rounds; clients never exchange raw data or raw predictions, only model\nweights, and the server only ever sees weighted averages.\n\nClient partitioning uses a Dirichlet distribution over classes, which is the standard way\nto simulate non-IID data across clients in the FL literature -- some clients end up\nattack-heavy, others mostly see legitimate traffic, which is a realistic stand-in for IoT\ndevices sitting in different parts of a network.\n\nTwo things in this version are worth calling out because they're easy to get wrong (an\nearlier draft of this pipeline got the first one wrong):\n\n**DP-SGD, not \"clip the aggregated update.\"** The earlier version of this notebook clipped\nand added noise to each client's *already-trained* weight delta, once, after local training\nfinished. That's not a mechanism anyone can put a privacy number on -- there's no accounting\ntheory that covers \"noise one vector at the end.\" Real differential privacy for SGD means\nclipping and noising the *gradient at every training step* (Abadi et al., 2016), which is\nwhat `local_train_step` below actually does, and which is what lets us call\n`dp_accounting`'s RDP accountant afterward and report a real (epsilon, delta).\n\nOne deliberate approximation, stated plainly rather than glossed over: per-step gradients\nare clipped and averaged over `DP_NUM_MICROBATCHES` groups per batch, not over every single\nexample individually. Full per-example clipping (`DP_NUM_MICROBATCHES = BATCH_SIZE`) is the\ntextbook version and is what we'd report as the headline number in a paper, but it means one\n`tf.GradientTape` call per example per batch, which is slow enough on Kaggle's CPU/GPU quota\nto make the ablation grid impractical. Grouped microbatching is the same relaxation\n`tensorflow-privacy`'s own examples use by default for exactly this reason -- it just means\nthe privacy unit is technically \"a group of a few records\" rather than \"one record,\" which\nis worth stating explicitly in the paper rather than letting a reviewer discover it.\n\n**FedProx as a second aggregation baseline.** Plain FedAvg can drift on non-IID data because\neach client's local model wanders toward its own local optimum before the server averages.\nFedProx (Li et al., 2020) adds a proximal term to the local loss that penalizes moving too\nfar from the last global model, which is a one-line change but a real, citable comparison\npoint rather than only ever reporting FedAvg.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"0ed62210-8cdd-414d-b216-c505eef57e04","cell_type":"code","source":"INPUT_DIM = X_train_bal.shape[1]\nNUM_CLASSES = len(label_encoder_classes)\n\n\ndef build_mlp(input_dim, num_classes):\n    model = keras.Sequential([\n        keras.layers.Input(shape=(input_dim,)),\n        keras.layers.Dense(128, activation=\"relu\"),\n        keras.layers.Dropout(0.2),\n        keras.layers.Dense(64, activation=\"relu\"),\n        keras.layers.Dense(num_classes, activation=\"softmax\"),\n    ])\n    model.compile(optimizer=keras.optimizers.Adam(learning_rate=1e-3),\n                  loss=\"sparse_categorical_crossentropy\", metrics=[\"accuracy\"])\n    return model\n\n\ndef dirichlet_partition(y, num_clients, alpha, seed):\n    rng = np.random.RandomState(seed)\n    classes = np.unique(y)\n    client_indices = [[] for _ in range(num_clients)]\n    for c in classes:\n        idx_c = np.where(y == c)[0]\n        rng.shuffle(idx_c)\n        proportions = rng.dirichlet(alpha=[alpha] * num_clients)\n        cut_points = (np.cumsum(proportions) * len(idx_c)).astype(int)[:-1]\n        for client_id, part in enumerate(np.split(idx_c, cut_points)):\n            client_indices[client_id].extend(part.tolist())\n    for c in client_indices:\n        rng.shuffle(c)\n    return [np.array(c) for c in client_indices]\n\n\ndef evaluate_keras_model(model, X, y, name=\"\"):\n    proba = model.predict(X, verbose=0)\n    preds = np.argmax(proba, axis=1)\n    acc = accuracy_score(y, preds)\n    f1_macro = f1_score(y, preds, average=\"macro\")\n    if name:\n        print(f\"{name}: accuracy={acc:.4f}  macro-F1={f1_macro:.4f}\")\n    return acc, f1_macro, preds, proba\n\n\ndef local_train_step(model, global_vars, x_batch, y_batch, loss_fn, optimizer,\n                      use_dp, dp_clip_norm, dp_noise_multiplier, num_microbatches,\n                      fedprox_mu):\n    \"\"\"One gradient step of local training, covering four combinations: plain SGD,\n    FedProx-only, DP-SGD-only, and DP-SGD+FedProx together. Kept as one function\n    rather than four near-duplicates, which is exactly the kind of duplication that\n    made the original notebook impossible to trust.\n    \"\"\"\n    def batch_loss(x, y):\n        preds = model(x, training=True)\n        loss = loss_fn(y, preds)\n        if fedprox_mu > 0:\n            prox = tf.add_n([\n                tf.reduce_sum(tf.square(v - gv))\n                for v, gv in zip(model.trainable_variables, global_vars)\n            ])\n            loss = loss + (fedprox_mu / 2.0) * prox\n        return loss\n\n    if not use_dp:\n        with tf.GradientTape() as tape:\n            loss = batch_loss(x_batch, y_batch)\n        grads = tape.gradient(loss, model.trainable_variables)\n        optimizer.apply_gradients(zip(grads, model.trainable_variables))\n        return\n\n    # DP-SGD: split the batch into microbatch groups, clip each group's gradient to a\n    # fixed L2 norm, average, then add Gaussian noise calibrated to that clip norm.\n    # This is what actually makes the per-step update satisfy a Gaussian-mechanism\n    # privacy guarantee -- clipping bounds each group's sensitivity, and the noise\n    # scale relative to that bound is what the RDP accountant below is accounting for.\n    n = x_batch.shape[0]\n    mb = min(num_microbatches, n)\n    groups = np.array_split(np.arange(n), mb)\n    summed_grads = None\n    for group in groups:\n        if len(group) == 0:\n            continue\n        with tf.GradientTape() as tape:\n            loss = batch_loss(tf.gather(x_batch, group), tf.gather(y_batch, group))\n        grads = tape.gradient(loss, model.trainable_variables)\n        flat_norm = tf.linalg.global_norm(grads)\n        scale = tf.minimum(1.0, dp_clip_norm / (flat_norm + 1e-12))\n        clipped = [g * scale for g in grads]\n        summed_grads = clipped if summed_grads is None else [s + g for s, g in zip(summed_grads, clipped)]\n\n    noised = [\n        (s + tf.random.normal(tf.shape(s), stddev=dp_clip_norm * dp_noise_multiplier)) / mb\n        for s in summed_grads\n    ]\n    optimizer.apply_gradients(zip(noised, model.trainable_variables))\n\n\ndef train_client_locally(model, global_weights, X, y, epochs, batch_size, seed,\n                          use_dp=False, dp_clip_norm=1.0, dp_noise_multiplier=0.0,\